    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes—once the heap starts to run low, even a small out‑of‑bounds write can turn the whole system chaotic. When you allocate many blocks, the free‑list becomes fragmented; the allocator stores bookkeeping data (size fields, next‑pointer links, guard bytes) in the gaps between blocks. If a stray write overwrites one of those metadata fields, the next `new`/`delete` will interpret corrupted data, causing:\n\n* **Double‑free or invalid‑free errors** that scramble the free‑list and make later allocations return overlapping pointers.  \n* **Allocation‑size corruption** that hands you a buffer that is shorter than you think, so a later write runs into the next block and corrupts unrelated data.  \n* **Heap‑wraparound** where the allocator thinks there is more free memory than actually exists, handing out pointers into memory that has already been reclaimed.\n\nThese failures are non‑deterministic because they depend on the exact order and size of previous allocations, which changes as the leak progresses.\n\n**Typical patterns to watch for**\n\n| Symptom | Likely cause | How it looks in logs |\n|---------|--------------|----------------------|\n| Random data in other structures after a few seconds |",
